Title: The adapter writes the work-counter nil down, so it cannot tell absent from never-looked
Created: 2026-09-03
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-03T12:49Z claude created

--- body ---

## Context

Found by re-running the acceptance layer at the v1.13.0 baseline (ticket 0618).
Three assertions rest on this and two standing rows cite them: R3's two clauses
and R13's duplicate-work clause all report `not-run` because "this target
reports no `work.<stage>.<trigger>.<outcome>` counters".

`bench/acceptance/adapters/zoteus.py` supplies that fact like this:

```python
# Explicitly None rather than absent. This target's status carries
# coverage and phase but no `work.<stage>.<trigger>.<outcome>` counter
# of any kind — measured on 2026-09-03, all 29 top-level keys read,
# no `work` or `counters` object anywhere. ...
"work": None,
```

The comment is honest about being a written-down measurement, and the
measurement was right on the day. The defect is that **the adapter answers
`None` whatever the target reports.** If upstream shipped a work counter
tomorrow, `durability.work_counters` would still return `None`, the three
assertions would still report `not-run`, and the two rows would still say the
counters do not exist. A check whose all-clear is reachable by failing to look
is not a check, and this is that shape in the instrument two `measured` rows
lean on.

The nil is *currently* true, and it was re-earned independently at the v1.13.0
re-baseline rather than inherited: the target's index status was read live on a
running server in the adapter's own default configuration, and it carries 28
top-level scalar keys with nothing matching `work` or `counter`. But that read
was done by hand, outside the adapter, and nothing in the tree repeats it.

**With a positive control, because a nil nobody could have seen is not a
finding.** Thirty of the seed's `passages.vector` blobs were nulled and the
status re-read: `passagesWithoutVectors` **does** appear, as a 29th key, with
the right value — and still no `work` or `counter` beside it. So the probe can
see a key arrive. That is what makes the nil a finding rather than a silence.

Two smaller things the same read establishes. The comment's "29 top-level keys"
is right for one state and wrong for the other: the count is 28 unless the index
has a vector shortfall. And `embedRate` is unreachable in any configuration the
layer may run, because upstream gates it on the provider being one of two API
providers and the default-configuration clause admits only the local embedder —
a source reading plus a measured absence, not a manufactured run.

## Actions

1. Read the target's status and derive `work` from what came back, so the nil is
   observed on every run rather than typed once.
2. Keep the distinction the comment is right about: `None` for "looked, found
   none" must stay distinguishable from a key the adapter forgot to populate.
3. Correct the key count, or drop it — a number in a comment that is right in
   one run state and wrong in another is the second copy this repository's own
   figure guard exists to refuse.

## Test

Red first: hand the adapter a status payload carrying a `work` object and
confirm the current code still answers `None`. That is the whole defect in one
assertion, and it fails today.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] `work` is derived from the target's reply on every run
- [ ] A target that reports counters makes R3's clauses decide instead of
      reporting `not-run`, demonstrated against a fixture that reports some
- [ ] The nil in `README.md`'s R3 and R13 rows rests on something that looks
      each time, not on a comment
